<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>New palpable left breast mass, clinically suspicious for cancer, in a postmenopausal woman of 52. Diagnostic mammography and ultrasound are booked this week, with core biopsy if indicated. Please see her once the results are available to discuss surgical options and axillary staging with her. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Urgent suspected-cancer pathway, within two weeks. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>If the biopsy confirms cancer, is she a candidate for breast-conserving surgery, and how would you present that choice and mastectomy to her?</item><item>Is breast MRI or any staging imaging needed before deciding?</item><item>How should her axilla be staged, and what would you do if the sentinel nodes are involved?</item><item>Should germline genetic testing be done before surgery?</item><item>What should she expect after surgery (radiation, endocrine therapy, and other systemic treatment), so that she can plan her work?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>A firm lump in the left breast she found two weeks ago. On examination a 2.5 cm hard, irregular, mobile mass at 2 o&apos;clock in the upper outer left breast, with no skin or nipple change and no axillary or supraclavicular nodes; right breast normal. A benign tumor was removed from the left breast about ten years ago. Menopause at 51; no hormone therapy; no pregnancies. A duodenal ulcer with Helicobacter pylori seven weeks ago, treated, now finishing omeprazole, so she should avoid nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. Mild knee osteoarthritis. Never smoker. No known allergy. She is a scientist and writer finishing a book. She asks to be told every result directly, in plain words, and to make her own decisions; please speak with her first and share information only with the people she names [S]</paragraph><list><item>A firm lump in the left breast she found two weeks ago. [S]</item><item>Wants to be told plainly and in person what any test shows, to see the reports herself, and to make her own decisions; asks that her health be shared only with the people she chooses. [S]</item><item>Worried that illness will keep her from finishing her book. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Rachel Louise Carson was born on May 27, 1907, on a family farm in Springdale, Pennsylvania. [D]</item><item>Carson was hired by the U.S. Bureau of Fisheries in 1936 as a junior aquatic biologist and rose to be editor-in-chief of the publications of its successor, the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service. She resigned in 1952 to write full time. [D]</item><item>In 1950, while preparing her second book, Carson had her first breast operation, to remove a benign tumor from her left breast. [D]</item><item>In early 1960, while writing Silent Spring, Carson found two lumps in her left breast. [D]</item></list><paragraph>10 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

